  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1220 PM CST Sat Nov 21 2026\n\nThe area of high pressure centered over the Mid-Atlantic that \nhas brought near- record warmth to the region will retreat to\nthe east ahead of an approaching surface cold front. The front\nis associated with an area of low pressure that will translate\nNE from the southern Plains to the Great Lakes between Saturday\nnight and Sunday night. \n\nRain will develop ahead of the cold front's passage, spreading \nfrom west to east across the area beginning early Sunday \nafternoon in southeast MO...and exiting southwest IN and the KY \nPennyrile by late Sunday night. Model ensemble guidance has been\nconsistent with showing about a 0.10-0.25\" of rain for the \nregion. However, with PWAT values around the 90th percentile, \nlocally higher totals closer to 0.50\" cannot be ruled out. \nAdditional with non-zero MUCAPE forecast over parts of SE \nMissouri into far western KY, a few rumbles of thunder are also \npossible. \n\nChilly Canadian high pressure will build southward into the\nUpper Midwest Monday and Tuesday, bringing a return of dry but\nchilly temperatures. High temperatures during this time look\nto struggle to climb much above 50 degrees. \n\nOn Wednesday, confidence continues to increase that light \nprecipitation will move across areas east of the Mississippi \nRiver. This will be in associated with an inverted H5 trough \nthat will lift NE from the Deep South into the Mid-Atlantic. \nPoPs have ramped up to 50-80%, with the highest values in the KY\nPennyrile. Despite this, forecast precipitation totals look to \nbe very light, only about a tenth of an inch at most. \nAdditionally, given the cold airmass, cannot rule out a few \nflurries mixing in with the light rain, but no accumulation or \nimpacts are expected. \n\nHigh pressure across the central Great Plains will move eastward\ninto the region on Friday. This will bring a return of dry\nweather and a gradual warming trend in temperatures.",
